<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Margraviate of Burgau</h1><p class="st-meta">1212 CE – 1805 CE (approx.) · capital Günzburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Swabian margraviate between Ulm and Augsburg that the Habsburgs held for five centuries as part of Further Austria, ruled from Günzburg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The margraves of Burgau, a branch of the counts of Berg, held lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindel">Mindel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunz">Günz</a> rivers from the early 13th century, using the margravial title from about 1212. When their line died out in 1301, the margraviate passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, who added it to their scattered Swabian possessions.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Burgau was a weak territory: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> often pledged it, and many monasteries, imperial cities and nobles inside its borders claimed their own rights, leaving the margrave chiefly with high justice and taxation. From 1609 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunzburg">Günzburg</a> was the residence of Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-margrave-of-burgau">Charles of Burgau</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-archduke-of-austria">Archduke Ferdinand II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-welser">Philippine Welser</a>. In the 18th century Günzburg housed an important Austrian mint.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria's</a> defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-pressburg-1805">Peace of Pressburg</a> of 1805 transferred Burgau to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>, soon the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>. Its lands now lie in the Bavarian district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Swabia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/margraviate-of-burgau.jpg" alt="Margraviate of Burgau" width="320" height="321"><figcaption>Image: Hagar66 based on work of TUBS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burgau_in_GZ.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1212 CE – 1805 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Günzburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles of Burgau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Burgau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1508&amp;focus=margraviate-of-burgau" aria-label="Margraviate of Burgau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4921 484 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Margraviate of Burgau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5241 740L5208 771M5241 740L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5208 771h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5241 740h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5241 740h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1508&amp;focus=margraviate-of-burgau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
